From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 22:18:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/12] Write a published header back before its journal is
 retired ("fsyncgate")

After a sync that fails with an I/O error, Linux marks the pages it could
not write back clean: the new header stays in the page cache, and a later
sync writes nothing. Header-journal recovery took such a header as
published and retired its journal behind that sync, leaving the old
header on the device; a power loss then discarded every later commit as a
stale WAL generation (SyncFailureRecovery_Tests: rows 11-15, acknowledged
durable, were lost). Recovery now makes the journal durable, writes the
header back as read (the same bytes; AES-ECB gives the same ciphertext),
and only then syncs and retires the journal. External review, point 3.

PromotionPowerLossScenario's cached-journal oracle now requires the data
file to stay byte-identical instead of counting writes: a valid header is
written back as it is, never replaced from the journal's copy.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UFnsmor6qyZMSd1VoKwfJh
